 Tuesday ✅.

 Today's newsletter is 915 words, a 3½-minute read. 

 🚨 Situational awareness: Space X and Tesla formed committees to explore a merger, CEO Elon Musk says.

 📈 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed up 0.3%. 

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: Braze (+51.7%), the customer-engagement platform, soared on the company's announced sale to Salesforce , with the software giant offering a 58% premium to Braze's stock at yesterday's close.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: SpaceX blasts past expectations 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Explosions are seldom good when it comes to a rocket company — unless, of course, you're referring to revenue or profit.

 🚀 SpaceX rocketed past expectations for both today in its second earnings report as a publicly traded company — with its space, connectivity and AI businesses all delivering strong performances. And in an explosive disclosure on its earnings call, Elon Musk said both SpaceX and Tesla have formed special committees to discuss a potential merger — with Musk saying, "It's a good idea." 📈 Zoom in: The company reported revenue of $14.58 billion in the third quarter, about $9 billion more than the same period last year.

 That crushed S&amp;P Capital IQ expectations of $13 billion. The company also swung from a $1.05 billion net loss a year earlier to a $4.4 billion profit in Q3, more than doubling consensus expectations of $2.12 billion. In all three of its divisions, SpaceX topped expectations, posting:

 🤖 AI revenue of $7.56 billion, nearly tripling its year-ago performance and beating S&amp;P Capital IQ consensus expectations of $6.47 billion. 🛰️ Connectivity revenue of $5.25 billion, topping expectations of $4.9 billion. 🚀 Space revenue of $1.77 billion, outpacing expectations of $1.46 billion. 💵 The company's capital expenditures totaled $19.59 billion in Q3, up from $6.88 billion a year earlier.

 Capex is expected to soar from $69.9 billion in 2026 to $194.3 billion in 2028, according to Melissa Otto, global head of Visible Alpha Research at S&amp;P Global. What we're watching: Musk said on this afternoon's call that he isn't on the special committees exploring a SpaceX-Tesla deal , as he's a "conflicted party."

 He said that "there's a lot to be gained" from cooperation on technologies like AI, chips, robotics and connectivity, but that a deal is not certain. "I think that would be overstating it," he said, noting that 50% of Tesla shareholders would need to approve a deal, which he called a high hurdle. Go deeper 

 
 
