 4. A test for the rollup playbook 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Antitrust officials are taking their fight to the playground... literally.

 The FTC and a dozen state attorneys general have sued to block a merger of two playground equipment makers — a case that could have broader implications for acquisition rollup strategies, Axios' Dan Primack writes . 🛝 State of play: The FTC and states argue that private equity-owned PlayPower's acquisition of rival BCI Burke would raise prices for playground equipment buyers — including schools, municipalities and churches — and decrease innovation. Simple enough.

 But the case — if it goes to court — could also test Biden-era merger guidelines that take particular aim at serial acquirers. 🔎 Zoom in: The guidelines allow regulators to "consider the cumulative effect" of a deal that's part of a broader acquisition strategy.

 PlayPower has made several big acquisitions over the past few decades. The guidelines also address mergers in already-concentrated markets:

 The top five playground equipment players — including PlayPower and BCI Burke — have combined market share of between 85% and 90%, the FTC argues. ⚖️ What we're watching: PlayPower believes the deal would create "even more competition in a highly fragmented and globally competitive market, not diminish it," a spokesperson says.

